{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB13365 - MONDO_2319",
  "verbalization": "Calcium Lactate Gluconate is implicated in the pathogenesis of Phosphorus Metabolism Disease through a series of indirect pathways involving Nephrocalcinosis. The drug is linked to Nephrocalcinosis, a condition characterized by the deposition of calcium salts in the kidneys, which in turn is associated with multiple calcium salts, including Calcium Acetate and Calcium Glycerophosphate. These calcium salts are indicated to contribute to Phosphorus Metabolism Disease, suggesting a potential role in disrupting normal phosphate homeostasis. The evidence suggests that the drug may influence phosphate metabolism through the accumulation of calcium salts, which could alter renal function and lead to metabolic disturbances. While the exact mechanism remains unclear, the consistent link between Calcium Lactate Gluconate and Phosphorus Metabolism Disease points to a possible dependency on calcium metabolism pathways, with the drug potentially acting as a mediator in the development of the disease.",
